► **ANSWER**

Looking at the failing tests, all involve `-u` and crash with SIGABRT/SIGTRAP (memory corruption). Let me examine the `dedup_inplace` function:

```c
if (best != n) {
    c->lines[n] = c->lines[best];  // copies struct including .data pointer
}
n++;
for (size_t k = i; k < j; k++) {
    if (k != best) free(c->lines[k].data);
}
```

**The bug:** When `n == i` (first group, or all prior groups had size 1) and `best > i`, we copy `c->lines[best]` into `c->lines[n]` (= `c->lines[i]`). Then the free loop hits `k == i`, sees `i != best`, and frees `c->lines[i].data` — which is now the **same pointer** as `c->lines[best].data` (we just copied the struct). This is a use-after-free.

This triggers with `-f -u` (where sort order by secondary byte comparison can place the earliest-input line later in the group) and with plain `-u` on inputs where the sorted order differs from input order for equal lines.

**Fix:** Save the kept line into a local, free all non-kept data in the group, *then* write the kept line to position `n`.

Here's the change to `dedup_inplace`:

```c
static void dedup_inplace(LineCollection *c) {
    size_t n = 0;
    size_t i = 0;
    while (i < c->count) {
        size_t best = i;
        size_t j = i + 1;
        while (j < c->count && lines_equal(&c->lines[i], &c->lines[j])) {
            if (c->lines[j].input_index < c->lines[best].input_index) {
                best = j;
            }
            j++;
        }
        Line kept = c->lines[best];
        for (size_t k = i; k < j; k++) {
            if (k != best) free(c->lines[k].data);
        }
        c->lines[n] = kept;
        n++;
        i = j;
    }
    c->count = n;
}
```

The difference: instead of copying the struct to `c->lines[n]` *before* the free loop (which can cause the free loop to free the just-copied data pointer), we:
1. Save the kept line into a local `Line kept`
2. Free all non-kept data in `[i, j)`
3. *Then* write `kept` into `c->lines[n]`

